  "text": "The study measured how quickly preschoolers responded to a sound, looking at how this speed related to gender, family background, mental ability, adult performance on the same task, personality type, and overall health. The participants were a hundred kindergarten children, split evenly between boys and girls, all about five and a half years old, and a group of twenty‑five young adult women averaging twenty‑three years of age. A simple device that emitted a tone and required a finger press was used; the experimenter held the main unit while the child faced the button. Each testing session included twenty‑five attempts, organized in five‑trial blocks with brief pauses, and a longer pause after the third block. Children could watch a clock during practice but not during the actual trials. Instructions emphasized keeping the finger ready to press, avoiding words that might cue hearing, so the response was meant to be mainly muscular, though some sensory influence was inevitable. Reliability checks showed high internal consistency (around .95 for children, .97 for adults) and test‑retest stability ranging from .78 to .90 for the children and .60 to .81 for the adults. The overall findings are summarized in the accompanying table."
